---
// Custom CHS case study. Replaces the Notion render. Notion still drives
// metadata (title, tags, cover, order) for the homepage list; only the
// body content is custom.
//
// Narrative spine: documentation IS product. The docs have to live where
// the work happens — and teach the tool by being the tool. Sections are
// moments of a non-technical employee at a 10,000-person co-op meeting an
// internal AI platform for the first time.

import CSLayout from "../../components/case-study/CSLayout.astro";
import CSHero from "../../components/case-study/CSHero.astro";
import CSSection from "../../components/case-study/CSSection.astro";
import CSPullquote from "../../components/case-study/CSPullquote.astro";
import CSStatGrid from "../../components/case-study/CSStatGrid.astro";
import CSLiveDemo from "../../components/case-study/CSLiveDemo.astro";
import CSFigure from "../../components/case-study/CSFigure.astro";
import ChsRolePicker from "../../components/case-study/showcase/ChsRolePicker.astro";
import ComprehensionSurface from "../../components/case-study/showcase/ComprehensionSurface.astro";

export const prerender = true;
---

<CSLayout
  title="CHS — Jon Tollefson"
  description="Teaching AI to 10,000 employees. The internal AI platform shipped — then the comprehension surface had to be designed. Sole UX function, embedded with engineering."
  ogImage="/case-studies/chs/cover.png"
>
  <CSHero
    eyebrow="Case study 02 · November 2025 — present"
    title="CHS"
    subtitle="Teaching AI to 10,000 employees."
    cover="/case-studies/chs/cover.png"
    meta={[
      { label: "Role", value: "Sole UX function, embedded with engineering" },
      { label: "Timeline", value: "Nov 2025 — present (launched Mar 2026)" },
      { label: "Stack", value: "Open WebUI · Azure · Figma" },
      { label: "Status", value: "Shipped · company-wide rollout in progress" },
    ]}
  />

  {/* ─────── 01 · COLD OPEN ─────── */}
  <CSSection num="01" title="You just got access" deck="A 10,000-person agricultural cooperative. The internal AI platform is live, and your name is on the list." noTopRule>
    <div class="cold-open" data-cold-open>
      <p class="cold-line" data-cold style="--i:0">The invite lands in your inbox.</p>
      <p class="cold-line" data-cold style="--i:1">You click the link. The platform opens.</p>
      <p class="cold-line" data-cold style="--i:2">It can build knowledge bases. Train custom models. Call functions. Query CHS data. All of it.</p>
      <p class="cold-line cold-line-beat" data-cold style="--i:3">You have no idea where to start.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="prose-block">
      <p>That blank moment — not the model — is the entire UX problem. Sales, GTM, finance, ops: smart people who should never need to know what an embedding is to get value from AI at work.</p>
      <p>I was the sole UX function on the platform team, embedded with engineering. The team's first reflex was <em>more docs</em>. Mine was different: the docs had to <em>teach</em> the tool by being the tool.</p>

      <CSPullquote hero>
        Documentation is product. When the first instinct for "users don't get it" is a Confluence page, you've already lost.
      </CSPullquote>
    </div>

    <CSStatGrid
      stats={[
        { n: 10000, label: "employees in the target audience", color: "white" },
        { n: 250, label: "beta cohort, pre-launch", color: "cyan" },
        { n: 3, label: "friction patterns surfaced", color: "yellow" },
        { n: 1, label: "comprehension surface to design", color: "pink" },
      ]}
    />
  </CSSection>

  {/* ─────── 02 · THE COMPREHENSION SURFACE ─────── */}
  <CSSection num="02" title="The comprehension surface" deck="Watch the gap open. Left: what the platform can do. Right: what a non-technical user actually asks. Nothing on one side answers the other.">
    <ComprehensionSurface />

    <p class="cs-caption-aside">Diagnosed from biweekly research with the pre-launch beta — the 250 employees already licensed for GPT Enterprise (power users in name, diagnostic cohort in practice). Three patterns: <strong>role disconnect</strong> (can't tell what it does for my job), <strong>tool confusion</strong> (how is this different from the GPT license I have?), and <strong>setup friction</strong> (custom KBs needed a back-channel ping to engineering — self-serve in name only).</p>

    <div class="prose-block">
      <p>The deliverable isn't a wiki. It's a set of interaction patterns living inside the tool, built so the docs and the platform are the same gesture. The rest of this is building that surface, piece by piece.</p>
    </div>
  </CSSection>

  {/* ─────── 03 · INVENTORY VS TASK FLOW ─────── */}
  <CSSection num="03" title="Features vs. tasks" deck="An inventory tells you what exists. A task flow tells you how to get something done. The platform shipped with only the first.">
    <CSFigure
      src="/case-studies/chs/notion-35fed8d4704f808d948ffbd7b51ca834.png"
      alt="Two columns side by side. Left, 'Feature inventory' for engineers: knowledge bases, models, functions, prompts, memory, tools, integrations. Right, 'Task flow' for non-technical users: set up your team's knowledge base, draft a proposal in CHS voice, query CHS data conversationally, summarize meeting notes, build a custom model. An arrow labeled 'reframe' connects the two."
      caption="Getting Started, rebuilt around tasks instead of features. Same platform, different organizing logic. The reframe arrow is the whole pedagogical move — and it ran on what the beta surfaced: where people stuck, the syntax they'd learned the hard way, the prompts that actually worked."
      width="wide"
      framed
    />
  </CSSection>

  {/* ─────── 04 · ROLE-AWARE START ─────── */}
  <CSSection num="04" title="It already speaks your job" deck="The sharpest friction in the beta: “I don't know what this is for, for me.” The fix — each role lands in a docs-chat whose starters are already in their own language. Pick a role. Then tap a starter and watch it run.">
    <CSLiveDemo
      label="LIVE · PICK A ROLE, RUN A STARTER"
      caption="Sales gets deal-review KBs and follow-up drafts. Finance gets variance summaries and board narratives. Ops gets SOP conversion and field-Spanish. Same platform — different introduction. Tap a starter and the assistant thinks, then streams back the kind of answer it actually returns."
      bleed
      pad="comfy"
    >
      <ChsRolePicker />
    </CSLiveDemo>
  </CSSection>

  {/* ─────── 05 · DOGFOOD THE DOCS ─────── */}
  <CSSection num="05" title="The docs are a working session" deck="The docs-chat uses the platform to teach the platform. Template prompts live in the chat. Embedded resources sit one click away.">
    <CSFigure
      src="/case-studies/chs/notion-35fed8d4704f8047b0fac992a9e05d5f.png"
      alt="The platform's docs assistant interface. A user message 'Set up a KB for my GTM team?' sits in the top right. Below, the docs assistant replies 'Let's walk through it. First, point the assistant at the files your team already uses.' followed by a highlighted embedded-resource pill: 'Template · embedded resource — Build a KB from our shared GTM Drive folder, tagged by quarter, with weekly auto-refresh.' Quick prompts at the bottom: 'Start a KB', 'Use a template', 'Compare with GPT Ent.' Two callouts: 'Template prompts live in the chat' and 'The chat uses the platform's own features to teach them.'"
      caption="The chat is a live session inside the platform. Every gesture learned here transfers verbatim to the user's own KB build the next morning — short-circuiting the gap between “read the manual” and “try it.”"
      width="wide"
      framed
    />

    <CSPullquote cite="Reflection, post-launch">
      The dogfooded docs-chat is the move I'd most repeat. Users learn the mental model by inhabiting it, not studying it.
    </CSPullquote>
  </CSSection>

  {/* ─────── 06 · WHAT SHIPPED ─────── */}
  <CSSection num="06" title="What shipped" deck="Since the March launch, departmental users are setting up custom models grounded in internal CHS databases — themselves, for the first time.">
    <CSStatGrid
      stats={[
        { n: 250, label: "beta cohort", suffix: " →", color: "white" },
        { n: 10000, label: "company-wide reach (rolling)", color: "green" },
        { n: 0, label: "back-channel pings to engineering needed", color: "cyan" },
        { n: 4, label: "interaction patterns shipped as the comprehension surface", color: "yellow" },
      ]}
    />

    <div class="prose-block">
      <p>Task-flow docs, role-aware starters, differentiation messaging, the dogfooded docs-chat — the four patterns are the platform's onboarding gesture now. Other internal teams have started inheriting them as a design system for their own surfaces on Open WebUI.</p>
    </div>
  </CSSection>

  {/* ─────── 07 · WHAT GENERALIZES — typographic statements ─────── */}
  <CSSection num="07" title="What generalizes" deck="Three things I'd carry into any enterprise AI tool with a non-technical audience." width="bleed">
    <div class="creed">
      <article class="creed-stmt" data-creed style="--i:0">
        <span class="creed-kicker">01</span>
        <p class="creed-line"><em>Documentation is product.</em></p>
        <p class="creed-sub">The docs have to live where the work happens. Same interaction patterns as the tool. If the docs and the tool look different, the docs are losing.</p>
      </article>

      <article class="creed-stmt" data-creed style="--i:1">
        <span class="creed-kicker">02</span>
        <p class="creed-line"><em>Teach by inhabiting.</em></p>
        <p class="creed-sub">A dogfooded docs-chat collapses “read the manual” into “try it.” Every gesture transfers verbatim to tomorrow's real build. The cheapest pedagogy you can buy.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </CSSection>

  {/* the closing image — full-bleed, dark, brand-gradient accent, nothing else */}
  <section class="closer" data-closer aria-label="The bottleneck is rarely the AI">
    <div class="closer-inner">
      <span class="closer-kicker">03 · the one to remember</span>
      <h2 class="closer-line">The bottleneck<br />is rarely<br /><span class="closer-grad">the AI.</span></h2>
      <p class="closer-sub">The model is fine. The bottleneck is the comprehension surface between capability and audience — and that gap is where senior design earns its keep.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <CSSection num="08" title="Coda" noTopRule width="narrow">
    <div class="prose-block">
      <p>The site you're reading is the same approach in miniature: Notion as content store, database rows as case-study variants, primitives reused across pages. The Tolo.Cloud case study takes it further; the Colophon documents how it's built.</p>
      <p class="muted">Specifics are kept high-level out of respect for ongoing project confidentiality. Happy to walk through the rest in conversation.</p>
    </div>
  </CSSection>

  <div class="cs-foot">
    <a href="/#work" class="cs-foot-back">← back to selected work</a>
  </div>
</CSLayout>

<script>
  // Scroll-gated reveals for the cold open, the creed statements, and the
  // closing image. One observer; each target adds .in once when it crosses
  // ~25% into view. prefers-reduced-motion → reveal immediately, no gate.
  // (The CSS already forces a final visible state under reduced motion; this
  //  just makes sure .in lands too so nothing depends on timing.)
  const reduce = window.matchMedia("(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)").matches;
  const targets = document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>(
    "[data-cold-open], [data-creed], [data-closer]"
  );

  if (reduce) {
    targets.forEach((t) => t.classList.add("in"));
  } else {
    const io = new IntersectionObserver(
      (entries, obs) => {
        entries.forEach((e) => {
          if (e.isIntersecting) {
            e.target.classList.add("in");
            obs.unobserve(e.target);
          }
        });
      },
      { threshold: 0.25 }
    );
    targets.forEach((t) => {
      io.observe(t);
      // If it's already on screen at load (e.g. the cold open above the fold),
      // reveal right away so it never sits invisible waiting for a scroll.
      const r = t.getBoundingClientRect();
      if (r.top < window.innerHeight * 0.85 && r.bottom > 0) {
        t.classList.add("in");
        io.unobserve(t);
      }
    });
  }
</script>

<style>
  .prose-block { max-width: 64ch; }
  .prose-block > .cs-pullquote { max-width: 64ch; }
  .prose-block .muted { color: rgba(245, 241, 232, 0.48); font-size: 14.5px; }

  /* Aside caption immediately under the inline comprehension surface.
     Matches the spacing of CSFigure captions so the visual rhythm
     stays the same as the rest of the case study. */
  .cs-caption-aside {
    font-family: ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, monospace;
    font-style: italic;
    font-size: 12.5px;
    color: rgba(245, 241, 232, 0.62);
    line-height: 1.6;
    margin: 14px 0 0;
    max-width: 80ch;
  }
  .cs-caption-aside strong {
    color: var(--cs-text);
    font-style: normal;
    font-weight: 700;
  }

  /* ── §01 cold open — a scene, lines fading in one at a time ── */
  .cold-open {
    max-width: 60ch;
    margin: 0 0 28px;
    display: grid;
    gap: 10px;
  }
  .cold-line {
    font-family: ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, monospace;
    font-size: clamp(16px, 2.2vw, 21px);
    line-height: 1.5;
    color: rgba(245, 241, 232, 0.82);
    margin: 0;
    max-width: none;
    opacity: 0;
    transform: translateY(8px);
    transition: opacity 520ms ease, transform 520ms ease;
  }
  .cold-open.in .cold-line {
    opacity: 1;
    transform: none;
    transition-delay: calc(var(--i) * 420ms);
  }
  .cold-line-beat {
    color: var(--brand-orange);
    font-style: italic;
    font-weight: 700;
    font-size: clamp(19px, 2.8vw, 26px);
    margin-top: 4px;
  }

  /* ── §07 creed — oversized mono-italic statements, one per breath ── */
  .creed {
    max-width: 1040px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    display: grid;
    gap: clamp(56px, 9vw, 96px);
    padding: 8px 0 12px;
  }
  .creed-stmt {
    opacity: 0;
    transform: translateY(20px);
    transition: opacity 700ms ease, transform 700ms ease;
  }
  .creed-stmt.in { opacity: 1; transform: none; }
  .creed-kicker {
    display: block;
    font-family: ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, monospace;
    font-size: 12px;
    letter-spacing: 0.28em;
    color: var(--brand-orange);
    margin-bottom: 14px;
  }
  .creed-line {
    font-family: ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, monospace;
    font-size: clamp(28px, 6vw, 64px);
    line-height: 1.05;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    color: #f5f1e8;
    margin: 0;
    max-width: none;
    overflow-wrap: break-word;
  }
  .creed-line em { font-style: italic; color: #f5f1e8; }
  .creed-sub {
    font-size: clamp(15px, 2.2vw, 18px);
    line-height: 1.55;
    color: rgba(245, 241, 232, 0.66);
    margin: 18px 0 0;
    max-width: 56ch;
  }

  /* ── the closing image — full-bleed, dark, brand-gradient accent ── */
  .closer {
    position: relative;
    width: 100vw;
    margin-left: 50%;
    transform: translateX(-50%);
    margin-top: clamp(72px, 12vw, 140px);
    margin-bottom: clamp(48px, 8vw, 96px);
    min-height: min(82vh, 760px);
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    background:
      radial-gradient(80% 70% at 50% 50%, rgba(255, 90, 60, 0.10) 0%, transparent 68%),
      #060509;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--cs-line);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--cs-line);
    overflow: hidden;
  }
  .closer-inner {
    max-width: 1160px;
    width: 100%;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: clamp(64px, 12vw, 140px) 32px;
    opacity: 0;
    transform: translateY(24px);
    transition: opacity 900ms ease, transform 900ms ease;
  }
  .closer.in .closer-inner { opacity: 1; transform: none; }
  .closer-kicker {
    display: block;
    font-family: ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, monospace;
    font-size: 11px;
    letter-spacing: 0.3em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: rgba(245, 241, 232, 0.5);
    margin-bottom: clamp(20px, 4vw, 36px);
  }
  .closer-line {
    font-family: ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, monospace;
    font-style: italic;
    font-weight: 700;
    font-size: clamp(38px, 11vw, 132px);
    line-height: 0.98;
    letter-spacing: -0.03em;
    color: #f5f1e8;
    margin: 0;
    max-width: none;
    overflow-wrap: break-word;
  }
  .closer-grad {
    background: var(--brand-grad);
    -webkit-background-clip: text;
    background-clip: text;
    color: transparent;
  }
  .closer-sub {
    font-family: ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, monospace;
    font-size: clamp(14px, 2vw, 17px);
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: rgba(245, 241, 232, 0.62);
    margin: clamp(28px, 5vw, 44px) 0 0;
    max-width: 60ch;
  }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .cold-line,
    .creed-stmt,
    .closer-inner {
      transition: none;
      opacity: 1;
      transform: none;
    }
    .cold-open.in .cold-line { transition-delay: 0ms; }
  }

  .cs-foot {
    text-align: center;
    padding: 64px 0 0;
  }
  .cs-foot-back {
    font-family: ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, monospace;
    font-size: 11px;
    letter-spacing: 0.22em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--brand-orange);
    text-decoration: none;
    padding: 8px 16px;
    border: 1px solid rgba(255, 90, 60, 0.32);
    border-radius: 2px;
    transition: color 160ms ease, border-color 160ms ease, background 160ms ease;
  }
  .cs-foot-back:hover {
    color: #f5f1e8;
    border-color: var(--brand-orange);
    background: rgba(255, 90, 60, 0.08);
  }
</style>
